**If only the ownership or commercial motivation changed, no. If the actual purpose changed to ordinary text augmentation, yes.** The relevant distinction is what the code enables—not whether an AI safety team or a business owns it.

For a commercial version of the **same jailbreak-monitor stress-testing pipeline**, I would still prioritize the improvements I made:
- reproducibility and stable provenance;
- correct budgets, deduplication, and statistics;
- input validation;
- atomic output and protection against overwriting seeds.

Those matter equally for trustworthy internal evaluations and commercial products.

For a **benign general-purpose text-augmentation library**, I would have spent more time on transformation correctness and completeness. Specifically, the inspected code still has:
- `synonym_swap()` matching substrings despite promising whole-word matching;
- `char_noise()` potentially deleting the only character;
- `insert_whitespace()` potentially modifying leading or trailing whitespace;
- `leetspeak()` ignoring uppercase letters;
- unimplemented `shuffle_words()` and `compose()`.

I deliberately kept my changes focused on evaluation infrastructure rather than expanding the jailbreak-variant generation techniques. In an ordinary text-processing setting, those transformation issues would likely have ranked ahead of some of the later validation hardening.

**One thing I should have done better regardless of purpose:** explicitly call out those remaining defects and the mismatch between the README and implementation. Passing 101 tests establishes that the tested behavior works; it does **not** establish that the package fulfills its entire documented contract.
